<section id="gateway">
<h2>Enable chat with explicit inputs.</h2>
<p>Use <a href="{{ source }}/charts/dekopon/values-pr-summarizer-linter.yaml">the release-pinned Slack/PR values example ↗</a> as a starting point. It does not install a GitHub component: add your reviewed <code>providers</code> or <code>providerSet</code> configuration. Replace its transport/model choices, identifiers and telemetry endpoints; check request budgets against the installed provider version.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Input comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Input</th>
<th scope="col">Chart value / owner</th>
<th scope="col">Required change</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Broker config and policy</th>
<td data-label="Chart value / owner">broker.config / broker.policies</td>
<td data-label="Required change">Map gateway UID 65533 separately from the 65532 probe; grant the sender and agent. Set the broker socket paths and policy files to chart mount paths.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Gateway config and catalog</th>
<td data-label="Chart value / owner">gateway.enabled: true; gateway.config; gateway.catalog</td>
<td data-label="Required change">Use the gatewayd API and configure a transport, model and catalogPath. Pin the broker server UID to 65532. Catalog is a direct ConfigMap mount.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Service/model credentials</th>
<td data-label="Chart value / owner">gateway.env / gateway.chatgpt</td>
<td data-label="Required change">Use Secret references for token environment variables; do not put token values in Helm values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Provider credentials/components</th>
<td data-label="Chart value / owner">broker.credentials / broker.providerSet</td>
<td data-label="Required change">Provide broker-only files. Install compatible components explicitly. Use the optional provider-sync hook or your own sync step; the pod precompiles the locked set before broker startup.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Model credentials stay with the gateway. Optional <a href="/inference/#budgets">token budgets and boot restore</a> limit model admission; the <a href="/inference/#proxy">guest model proxy</a> needs its own mTLS listener and trusted jail. None is enabled by an application upgrade.</p>
<p>For WhatsApp or another inbound webhook, enable <code>gateway.service</code>, bind the configured listener to <code>0.0.0.0</code> on the same port, and provide your own exact-path ingress/TLS route. Slack and Discord connect outbound; the broker’s provider HTTP is outbound too. The chart does not install a network egress policy.</p>
<p>Render the combined values before upgrade, then repeat the rollout/probe checks. Finally send a chat message that invokes a harmless granted capability and <a href="/guides/traces/#records">check decision and execution records</a>; socket readiness alone does not prove the model, chat service or provider credentials work.</p>
</section>
<section id="providers">
<h2>Install the provider set before the broker starts.</h2>
<p>The application image includes only <code>cli-probe</code> and <code>http-probe</code>. GitHub, curl, Python, memory and other out-of-tree components need explicit, reviewed installation. A <code>providers:</code> path list and a managed <code>providerSet:</code> are mutually exclusive.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Provider installation stages">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">What the operator must arrange</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Sync</th><td data-label="What the operator must arrange"><code>provider sync</code> writes the immutable lock and source blobs. Enable <code>broker.providerSet.enabled</code> and either populate its state-claim subdirectory yourself or use <code>broker.providerSync.enabled</code> with a <code>providers.yaml</code> ConfigMap.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Precompile</th><td data-label="What the operator must arrange">The chart’s <code>precompile</code> init container runs after <code>prepare-files</code>, as broker UID 65532 with the broker image and resource limits. It checks locked local blobs, fills or repairs compiled artifacts, and prunes stale cache generations.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Load</th><td data-label="What the operator must arrange">The broker verifies and maps the compiled cache; it refuses a missing or invalid entry. It reads the lock at startup, so a changed lock needs a pod rollout. An external ConfigMap change does not trigger one automatically.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The default Helm pre-install/pre-upgrade sync hook needs an existing claim and ConfigMap: set <code>state.existingClaim</code> before the first install. Argo CD needs an explicit Sync-hook/wave order so those inputs exist before the Job. <a href="{{ source }}/docs/kubernetes.md#syncing-the-set-with-the-charts-hook-job">Hook setup and ordering ↗</a>.</p>
<p>Outside the chart, run this as the broker’s UID after syncing the set and after each application upgrade, before starting the managed broker. Replace both paths with your configured paths:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Precompile a managed provider set"><code>dekopon-brokerd provider \
  --lock-file /var/lib/dekopon/providers/providers.lock.yaml \
  --store /var/lib/dekopon/providers/store precompile</code>
</pre>
<p>Direct <code>providers:</code> paths compile from source. <code>compileOnLoad: true</code> bypasses the managed cache, but does not skip the chart’s precompile init container. Stop the broker before manually removing mapped artifacts.</p>
<p><strong>Check component imports, not just version labels.</strong> The current broker requires <code>dekopon:provider@0.4.0</code> and refuses old <code>provider@0.3.0</code> components. HTTP <code>client@1.2.0</code> imports are also refused; use the supported <code>client@1.1.0</code> contract. <a href="/guides/provider-sdk/">Provider SDK and compatibility</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="files">
<h2>Projected Secrets become private ordinary files.</h2>
<p>Kubernetes Secret projections contain symlinks; the daemon’s ordinary credential loader requires owned regular files. <code>prepare-files</code> copies them to the correct daemon’s private volume at mode <code>0600</code>, checking ownership and paths rather than handing both processes a common config directory.</p>
<p>For split configuration, use <code>broker.configDirectory.configMap</code> or <code>gateway.configDirectory.configMap</code> and clear the conflicting single-file values. Set the corresponding <code>drainBudget.assumedBrokerShutdownGraceMs</code> / <code>assumedGatewayShutdownGraceMs</code>; the chart cannot read a grace period inside an external directory. Chart-rendered <code>peers.yaml</code> maps the probe and gateway separately, and must not collide with a supplied fragment.</p>
<p>Changes to copied configuration or credential inputs require a pod rollout; use your own watched-secret restart mechanism. Inline chart config changes have checksum-triggered rollouts. Do not add <code>fsGroup</code>: group-readable credential files are refused.</p>
<div id="credentials">
<h3>Rotating credentials are seeded once.</h3>
<p>ChatGPT gateway and broker credentials use different token families and different persistent subdirectories. The live refresh token rotates; the original exported seed is not a backup. Restoring it over the live file can revoke access. Use <code>reseed</code> only after deliberate re-login, then turn it back off.</p>
<p>Optional <code>broker.secretMap</code> and bootstrap files stay broker-only. Live Kubernetes token sources use <code>broker.secretSourceVolumes</code> and their dedicated projection adapter; do not init-copy a rotating ServiceAccount token or mount it with <code>subPath</code>. OAuth refresh records need a broker-owned writable persistent directory. <a href="/guides/access/#credentials">Native credential handling</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="state">
<h2>Retained state is separate from temporary assets.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Storage comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Storage</th>
<th scope="col">Ownership and lifecycle</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">State PVC</th>
<td data-label="Ownership and lifecycle">Retained on uninstall by default; daemon-private rotating credentials, optional managed-provider store, and opted-in gateway journal/wakes. Back up live state, not old credential seeds. The default local-path class may not support expansion.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Provider-storage PVC</th>
<td data-label="Ownership and lifecycle">Optional, broker-only and always retained when chart-created. This is physically separate from model-credential state. Direct writes are not invocation transactions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Gateway scratch / broker assets</th>
<td data-label="Ownership and lifecycle">Separate disk-backed emptyDir volumes, each 320Mi by default. Gateway live retention defaults to 256 MiB; broker in-flight assets to 64 MiB. Losing the pod loses retained chat assets.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Config / socket / broker scratch</th>
<td data-label="Ownership and lifecycle">Separate memory-backed emptyDir volumes; no durable audit archive lives here.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The chart mounts the broker asset volume but does not rewrite <code>broker.yaml</code>. Add this to your custom broker configuration to enable output allocation and streamed HTTP; keep the path and budgets aligned with chart values:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Broker asset configuration excerpt"><code>assets:
  rootPath: /var/lib/dekopon-assets
  maxInFlightBytes: 67108864</code>
</pre>
<p>Increase volume size separately if you raise typed retention or in-flight budgets. Unlinked broker outputs remain on that disk until the gateway closes its descriptors. <a href="/guides/runtime/#assets">Decoded-content limits are separate</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="startup">
<h2>Find the startup stage that stopped.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Symptom comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Symptom</th>
<th scope="col">Check</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Init never completes</th>
<td data-label="Check">PVC binding, root-init admission and file ownership. Inspect prepare-files logs without publishing credential contents; if precompile stopped, check the locked blobs, ownership, image version and memory limit.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Broker never becomes ready</th>
<td data-label="Check">Provider compatibility/loading, policy/config parsing and socket ownership. Startup gets 60 attempts at 5-second intervals.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Gateway has not started</th>
<td data-label="Check">With gateway enabled, the broker is a native sidecar; its startup probe must pass before gateway launch.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Webhook Service has no ready endpoint</th>
<td data-label="Check">Broker readiness plus gateway TCP readiness; listener must match port and bind beyond loopback.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Shutdown cuts off work</th>
<td data-label="Check">Gateway drains before broker. Default 270-second pod grace covers two 120-second drains plus 30 seconds; do not budget only the longer daemon.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>There is no liveness probe that silently restarts an in-flight broker. A compromised gateway still speaks for its entire configured attestor lane; process separation does not authenticate individual chat users by itself.</p>
